{"slug":"how-to-buy-glp1-legally-usa","title":"How to buy GLP-1 medicines legally in the USA","desc":"The legal ways to buy or order Wegovy, Zepbound, Ozempic, Mounjaro and GLP-1 pills in the US — prescriptions, pharmacies, telehealth and direct-from-manufacturer.",
 "body":"""<p>Every GLP-1 medicine is prescription-only in the US. There is no legal way to purchase them without a valid prescription from a licensed prescriber.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Get a prescription</h2><p>From your own doctor or from a telehealth service whose prescribers are licensed in your state. A real consultation reviews your medical history, current medicines and any personal or family history of thyroid cancer.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Choose where to fill it</h2><ul>
<li><strong>Your local or mail-order pharmacy</strong>, billed to insurance when covered.</li>
<li><strong>Direct-from-manufacturer self-pay</strong>: NovoCare Pharmacy (Wegovy, Ozempic, Wegovy pill) and LillyDirect (Zepbound, Mounjaro, Foundayo).</li>
<li><strong>Telehealth programs</strong> that send prescriptions to licensed pharmacies.</li></ul>
<h2>Step 3: Check the pharmacy is legitimate</h2><p>Use the NABP's <a href="https://safe.pharmacy/" rel="nofollow noopener">safe.pharmacy</a> lookup. Legitimate online pharmacies require a prescription, are licensed in your state and have a US address.</p>
<h2>Red flags</h2><ul><li>No prescription required</li><li>"Research use only" peptides or powders</li><li>Payment by crypto, gift card or wire only</li><li>Ordering through WhatsApp or social media DMs</li><li>Pens labeled for another country</li></ul>
<p>In August 2026 the FDA issued warning letters to online sellers of products marketed as semaglutide, tirzepatide and retatrutide. Foreign-labeled pens are unapproved in the US even when genuine.</p>"""},
{"slug":"how-to-buy-glp1-legally-canada","title":"How to buy GLP-1 medicines legally in Canada","desc":"How to get Ozempic, Wegovy, Mounjaro and generic semaglutide legally in Canada: prescriptions, pharmacies, coverage and the new generics.",
 "body":"""<p>In Canada, GLP-1 medicines are prescription drugs dispensed by provincially licensed pharmacies.</p>
<h2>Get a prescription</h2><p>From a family doctor, nurse practitioner, or a licensed virtual-care provider in your province.</p>
<h2>Generic semaglutide is here</h2><p>Health Canada authorized the first generic semaglutide (Ozempic equivalent) on April 28, 2026 and a second on May 1, 2026, and the first generic for weight management on June 29, 2026. Ask your pharmacist whether a generic can be substituted under your plan and province.</p>
<h2>Verify the pharmacy</h2><p>Every pharmacy must be licensed by its provincial regulatory college. Look it up on your college's public register before ordering online.</p>
<h2>Coverage</h2><p>Provincial plans and private insurers set their own rules; many require prior authorization. Obesity coverage is often an optional benefit.</p>"""},
{"slug":"glp1-cost-without-insurance","title":"GLP-1 cost without insurance (US, 2026)","desc":"What Wegovy, Zepbound, Ozempic, Mounjaro, the Wegovy pill and Foundayo cost without insurance through NovoCare and LillyDirect in 2026.",
 "body":"""<p>Self-pay prices changed several times in 2025–2026. The figures below were reported by manufacturers and trade press and should be verified on the official pages before you fill a prescription.</p>
<table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Medicine</th><th>Self-pay channel</th><th>Reported monthly price</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Wegovy injection 0.25–2.4 mg</td><td>NovoCare, partner pharmacies</td><td>About $349 (intro offers on starter doses)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wegovy HD 7.2 mg</td><td>NovoCare</td><td>About $399</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wegovy pill</td><td>NovoCare</td><td>From $149 (starter) to about $299</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ozempic</td><td>NovoCare, partner pharmacies</td><td>About $349; higher for 2 mg</td></tr>
<tr><td>Zepbound vials</td><td>LillyDirect</td><td>$299 (2.5 mg), $399 (5 mg), $449 (higher)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Foundayo</td><td>LillyDirect</td><td>$149 / $199 / $299 by dose</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>With commercial insurance that covers the drug, manufacturer savings cards can bring copays as low as $25/month. Medicare arrangements announced in 2025 set lower copays for eligible patients; check current Medicare rules.</p>"""},
{"slug":"compounded-and-counterfeit-glp1","title":"Compounded, research-peptide and counterfeit GLP-1s: the risks","desc":"Why compounded, 'research' and counterfeit semaglutide and tirzepatide are risky, and how to spot fake Ozempic and Wegovy pens.",
 "body":"""<p>Compounded semaglutide and tirzepatide are not FDA-approved. Products sold as "research peptides" are not meant for humans at all. Counterfeit pens of Ozempic and other GLP-1s have been found in the US and abroad.</p>
<h2>How to protect yourself</h2><ul><li>Fill prescriptions only at licensed pharmacies.</li><li>Check pen labels, lot numbers and expiry dates; report anything odd to the pharmacist.</li><li>Never buy from social media sellers, WhatsApp vendors or sites that don't need a prescription.</li><li>Report suspected fakes to FDA MedWatch (US) or Health Canada.</li></ul>
<p>Sellers of these products face FDA warning letters and lawsuits from Novo Nordisk and Eli Lilly.</p>"""},
{"slug":"glp1-side-effects","title":"GLP-1 side effects: what's common, what's serious","desc":"Common and serious side effects of semaglutide, tirzepatide, liraglutide and orforglipron, and when to call a doctor.",
 "body":"""<p>Most side effects are digestive and appear when starting or increasing the dose: nausea, diarrhea, vomiting, constipation and stomach pain. Slow dose increases are designed to limit them.</p>
<h2>Get medical help for</h2><ul><li>Severe stomach pain that does not go away, with or without vomiting (possible pancreatitis)</li><li>Pain in the upper right abdomen, fever, yellow skin or eyes (gallbladder)</li><li>Signs of dehydration or reduced urine</li><li>A lump or swelling in the neck, hoarseness, trouble swallowing</li><li>Signs of a serious allergic reaction</li></ul>
<p>Tell any surgical or endoscopy team that you take a GLP-1 medicine, because of the risk of aspiration under anesthesia.</p>"""},
{"slug":"glp1-pipeline-2026","title":"New GLP-1 drugs coming: the 2026–2027 pipeline","desc":"CagriSema, retatrutide and other upcoming obesity and diabetes drugs: status, results and expected decisions.",
 "body":"""<ul><li><strong>CagriSema</strong> (Novo Nordisk): filed with the FDA December 2025; decision expected Q4 2026.</li>
<li><strong>Retatrutide</strong> (Lilly): triple agonist; FDA submission planned for Q1 2027 after positive phase 3 results.</li>
<li><strong>Ozempic tablets 25 mg</strong>: under FDA review, decision expected by end of 2026.</li>
<li><strong>Survodutide</strong> (Boehringer Ingelheim / Zealand Pharma) and other agents remain in development.</li></ul>
<p>None of the investigational products is legally sold. Anything marketed as one online is unapproved.</p>"""},
